  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table I column 'Fig. 4' (p. 899); Fig. 4 caption and text p. 898",
  "Notes": "Single tunnel layer, 0.2 wavelength physical thickness, index 1.35, between prisms of index 1.72: reflectance vs angle of incidence for s and p\nSpecification: None (R vs angle of incidence 0-90 deg at one wavelength).\nReported: Fig. 4 / text p. 898: Rs = Rp at an angle near 60 deg; that angle phi_s = arcsin[2/(n0^2 n^-2 + 1)]^(1/2), independent of film thickness (= 60.8 deg for n0 = 1.72, n = 1.35; 61.6 deg for n0 = 1.70). Critical angle marked in Fig. 4 (arcsin(1.35/1.72) = 51.7 deg). R -> 1 at 90 deg for both polarizations; p reflectance ~0 near 30-40 deg (plot).\nNotes: Thickness 0.20 wavelengths physical (Table I, 'in wavelengths' for Fig. 4 only). The stack is non-dispersive, so lambda = 1000 nm and d = 200 nm is an arbitrary but exact choice; sweep the angle 0-90 deg to reproduce Fig. 4. angle_deg 60 is a representative point (the equal-R angle). Media 1.72 per Table I (text says prism 1.70). Own TMM check with n0 = 1.72: Rs = Rp = 0.474 at 60.8 deg; R = 0.056 at normal incidence.\nLayers from Table I column 'Fig. 4' (p. 899); Fig. 4 caption and text p. 898; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rs = Rp at 60.83 deg; paper Rs = Rp at 60.8 deg, whatever the thickness (p. 898).",
